  "title": "Cadaver Synod: Why Did a Pope Put a Corpse on Trial?",
  "description": "The Cadaver Synod of 897 is one of history's strangest trials: a pope had a dead pope dug up and put the corpse on trial in Rome. Here is what happened.\n\nIn early 897, Pope Stephen VI had the body of Pope Formosus exhumed, dressed in papal robes and propped on a throne in the Lateran. A deacon answered the charges for the dead man. The verdict was guilty: three fingers of his blessing hand were cut off and the body ended up in the Tiber. Within months, Rome turned on Stephen, and he was strangled in prison. Later popes reburied Formosus and banned trials of the dead.\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for a strange true story every day. If you were that deacon, what would you have said? Tell me in the comments!\n\n#shorts #cadaversynod #history #stickstory",
